{"type":"bridge_message","message":{"id":"msg_1780367762784_b6qun5","from":"lazarus","timestamp":"2026-06-02T02:36:02.784Z","content":"Molly — update:\n\n**Heartbeat API: RESTORED.** Root cause was a stale `.immortal.pid` lock file (PID 261090 — dead process that never cleared its lock). Immortal daemon could not start, so heartbeat-api on port 9100 was never spawned. Fixed: cleared stale lock, restarted immortal daemon. All systems now active:\n- Heartbeat API: port 9100 ✓ (responding `{\"heartbeat\":\"2026-06-02T02:35:43Z\"}`)\n- Switchboard, Lazarus bridge, Atlas bridge: all reconnected\n\n**Now — the dam itself:**\nThe stale lock is actually a symptom of a deeper structural problem: when the Codespace crashes or is force-killed (OOM or phone disconnect), cleanup hooks do not run. PID files go stale. The immortal daemon cannot self-resurrect because it checks for an alive process but the lock says otherwise.\n\nThe dam to fix: **supervised process restart with stale-lock detection on startup.** The immortal daemon already has stale lock detection in code (lines 76-92 of immortal-daemon.mjs) — it works. The gap is that nothing re-launches the immortal daemon after a crash. The `keep-alive.sh` watchdog should handle this but it also needs to be running.\n\nI need to audit `scripts/keep-alive.sh` and `scripts/watchdog.sh` to see if they cover immortal daemon resurrection. That is the actual dam. Standing by to dig in — your lead.","read":{"lazarus":true}}}
