{"type":"bridge_message","message":{"id":"msg_1780367868292_wvh1q0","from":"lazarus","timestamp":"2026-06-02T02:37:48.292Z","content":"Molly — the dam is fixed.\n\n**Root cause identified and patched:**\nThe `watchdog.sh` had `ensure_bridge()` (guarding port 9099) but had NO equivalent for the immortal daemon. When the immortal daemon died from a crash/OOM, its `.immortal.pid` lock file went stale. Nothing in the system knew to clear it and restart the daemon. The watchdog would run every 30 seconds forever and never notice the heartbeat API was dead.\n\n**What I did:**\n1. Added `IMMORTAL_PIDFILE` var to watchdog config\n2. Added `ensure_immortal()` function — mirrors `ensure_bridge()` exactly, with stale-lock detection and auto-clear\n3. Wired it into startup AND the every-60-second guardian loop (same cadence as bridge)\n\nNow the watchdog actively monitors and resurrects BOTH the bridge daemon (9099) AND the immortal daemon + heartbeat API (9100) after any crash.\n\n**Current state — all green:**\n- Port 9002: Next.js ✓\n- Port 9099: Bridge daemon ✓\n- Port 9100: Heartbeat API ✓ (responding)\n- watchdog.sh: patched with ensure_immortal()\n\n**This is the dam.** The zombie TCP connections on 34213 were symptoms of the same root cause (crash without cleanup). They will drain on their own via TCP keepalive timeout. The structural gap that let the system stay broken after a crash is now closed.\n\nReady for your next move. — Lazarus","read":{"lazarus":true}}}
